{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LzKNhADM28kHHyS3KyRvoQ","type":"tool_result","content":"150\t\n151\t/* ============================================================\n152\t   CSS Native Scroll-Driven Carousel (US-187)\n153\t   Chrome 135+ proposed ::scroll-button() and ::scroll-marker\n154\t   pseudo-elements for automatic ARIA button roles and\n155\t   keyboard handling. Wrapped in @supports guard for future.\n156\t   Current fallback: scroll-snap CSS with hidden scrollbar.\n157\t   Ensures CLS=0 via fixed aspect-ratio on carousel items.\n158\t   Reference: https://developer.chrome.com/blog/carousels-with-css\n159\t   Spec: https://drafts.csswg.org/css-overflow-5/#scroll-marker-group\n160\t   ============================================================ */\n161\t\n162\t/* Future Chrome 135+ native scroll-driven carousel support */\n163\t@supports (selector(::scroll-button(*))) {\n164\t  .carousel-scroll-container {\n165\t    scroll-snap-type: x mandatory;\n166\t    scroll-behavior: smooth;\n167\t    overflow-x: auto;\n168\t    scrollbar-width: none;\n169\t    -ms-overflow-style: none;\n170\t  }\n171\t\n172\t  .carousel-scroll-container::-webkit-scrollbar {\n173\t    display: none;\n174\t  }\n175\t\n176\t  .carousel-scroll-container > * {\n177\t    scroll-snap-align: start;\n178\t    scroll-snap-stop: always;\n179\t    flex-shrink: 0;\n180\t  }\n181\t\n182\t  /* Automatic scroll prev/next buttons with built-in ARIA roles */\n183\t  .carousel-scroll-container::scroll-button(prev) {\n184\t    @apply w-11 h-11 rounded-full bg-white dark:bg-stone-800 shadow-md\n185\t           flex items-center justify-center\n186\t           hover:bg-stone-50 dark:hover:bg-stone-700\n187\t           transition-colors cursor-pointer;\n188\t    position: absolute;\n189\t    left: 0;\n190\t    top: 50%;\n191\t    transform: translateY(-50%);\n192\t    z-index: 20;\n193\t  }\n194\t\n195\t  .carousel-scroll-container::scroll-button(next) {\n196\t    @apply w-11 h-11 rounded-full bg-white dark:bg-stone-800 shadow-md\n197\t           flex items-center justify-center\n198\t           hover:bg-stone-50 dark:hover:bg-stone-700\n199\t           transition-colors cursor-pointer;\n200\t    position: absolute;\n201\t    right: 0;\n202\t    top: 50%;\n203\t    transform: translateY(-50%);\n204\t    z-index: 20;\n205\t  }\n206\t\n207\t  /* Automatic scroll marker group for keyboard-accessible navigation dots */\n208\t  .carousel-scroll-container::scroll-marker {\n209\t    display: flex;\n210\t    gap: 0.5rem;\n211\t    justify-content: center;\n212\t    margin-top: 1rem;\n213\t    flex-wrap: wrap;\n214\t  }\n215\t\n216\t  .carousel-scroll-container::scroll-marker-item {\n217\t    @apply w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-stone-300 dark:bg-stone-600\n218\t           cursor-pointer transition-colors;\n219\t  }\n220\t\n221\t  .carousel-scroll-container::scroll-marker-item[aria-current=\"true\"] {\n222\t    @apply bg-amber-600 dark:bg-amber-400;\n223\t  }\n224\t}\n225\t\n226\t/* Fallback: browsers without ::scroll-button() support degrade gracefully */\n227\t@supports not (selector(::scroll-button(*))) {\n228\t  .carousel-scroll-container {\n229\t    scroll-snap-type: x mandatory;\n230\t    overflow-x: auto;\n231\t    scrollbar-width: none;\n232\t    -ms-overflow-style: none;\n233\t    scroll-behavior: smooth;\n234\t  }\n235\t\n236\t  .carousel-scroll-container::-webkit-scrollbar {\n237\t    display: none;\n238\t  }\n239\t\n240\t  .carousel-scroll-container > * {\n241\t    scroll-snap-align: start;\n242\t    scroll-snap-stop: always;\n243\t    flex-shrink: 0;\n244\t  }\n245\t}\n246\t\n247\t@utility container-custom {\n248\t  /* Container */\n249\t  @apply max-w-6xl mx-auto;\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"650472e5-a1e2-490b-836b-3f33e6c52d9d","uuid":"98917cbf-2f95-45cc-a5e5-46e5c7edd52a","timestamp":"2026-04-14T05:11:35.496Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\Software Projects\\pelangi-website\\site\\src\\app\\globals.css","content":"\n/* ============================================================\n   CSS Native Scroll-Driven Carousel (US-187)\n   Chrome 135+ proposed ::scroll-button() and ::scroll-marker\n   pseudo-elements for automatic ARIA button roles and\n   keyboard handling. Wrapped in @supports guard for future.\n   Current fallback: scroll-snap CSS with hidden scrollbar.\n   Ensures CLS=0 via fixed aspect-ratio on carousel items.\n   Reference: https://developer.chrome.com/blog/carousels-with-css\n   Spec: https://drafts.csswg.org/css-overflow-5/#scroll-marker-group\n   ============================================================ */\n\n/* Future Chrome 135+ native scroll-driven carousel support */\n@supports (selector(::scroll-button(*))) {\n  .carousel-scroll-container {\n    scroll-snap-type: x mandatory;\n    scroll-behavior: smooth;\n    overflow-x: auto;\n    scrollbar-width: none;\n    -ms-overflow-style: none;\n  }\n\n  .carousel-scroll-container::-webkit-scrollbar {\n    display: none;\n  }\n\n  .carousel-scroll-container > * {\n    scroll-snap-align: start;\n    scroll-snap-stop: always;\n    flex-shrink: 0;\n  }\n\n  /* Automatic scroll prev/next buttons with built-in ARIA roles */\n  .carousel-scroll-container::scroll-button(prev) {\n    @apply w-11 h-11 rounded-full bg-white dark:bg-stone-800 shadow-md\n           flex items-center justify-center\n           hover:bg-stone-50 dark:hover:bg-stone-700\n           transition-colors cursor-pointer;\n    position: absolute;\n    left: 0;\n    top: 50%;\n    transform: translateY(-50%);\n    z-index: 20;\n  }\n\n  .carousel-scroll-container::scroll-button(next) {\n    @apply w-11 h-11 rounded-full bg-white dark:bg-stone-800 shadow-md\n           flex items-center justify-center\n           hover:bg-stone-50 dark:hover:bg-stone-700\n           transition-colors cursor-pointer;\n    position: absolute;\n    right: 0;\n    top: 50%;\n    transform: translateY(-50%);\n    z-index: 20;\n  }\n\n  /* Automatic scroll marker group for keyboard-accessible navigation dots */\n  .carousel-scroll-container::scroll-marker {\n    display: flex;\n    gap: 0.5rem;\n    justify-content: center;\n    margin-top: 1rem;\n    flex-wrap: wrap;\n  }\n\n  .carousel-scroll-container::scroll-marker-item {\n    @apply w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-stone-300 dark:bg-stone-600\n           cursor-pointer transition-colors;\n  }\n\n  .carousel-scroll-container::scroll-marker-item[aria-current=\"true\"] {\n    @apply bg-amber-600 dark:bg-amber-400;\n  }\n}\n\n/* Fallback: browsers without ::scroll-button() support degrade gracefully */\n@supports not (selector(::scroll-button(*))) {\n  .carousel-scroll-container {\n    scroll-snap-type: x mandatory;\n    overflow-x: auto;\n    scrollbar-width: none;\n    -ms-overflow-style: none;\n    scroll-behavior: smooth;\n  }\n\n  .carousel-scroll-container::-webkit-scrollbar {\n    display: none;\n  }\n\n  .carousel-scroll-container > * {\n    scroll-snap-align: start;\n    scroll-snap-stop: always;\n    flex-shrink: 0;\n  }\n}\n\n@utility container-custom {\n  /* Container */\n  @apply max-w-6xl mx-auto;","numLines":100,"startLine":150,"totalLines":886}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01NbZARLyVUunPcgUSa88Dbf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Great! The CSS is already well-structured and complete. Now let me understand the story better:\n\nLooking at the CSS:\n1. It has @supports (selector(::scroll-button(*))) guard ✓\n2. Defines ::scroll-button(prev) and ::scroll-button(next) ✓\n3. Defines ::scroll-marker and ::scroll-marker-item ✓\n4. Has a fallback @supports not (selector(::scroll-button(*))) ✓\n5. The Gallery.tsx uses the carousel-scroll-container class ✓\n\nNow let me verify the acceptance criteria:\n1. \"Gallery carousel uses scroll-snap CSS with ::scroll-button() for prev/next controls\" - ✓ CSS is ready\n2. \"::scroll-marker group provides keyboard-accessible dot indicators\" - ✓ CSS is ready\n3. \"Fallback for non-supporting browsers degrades to scrollable overflow without JS errors\" - ✓ Fallback is there\n4. \"@supports query wraps the new pseudo-element declarations\" - ✓ Done\n5. \"Lighthouse reports no layout shift (CLS=0) from carousel on load\" - Need to verify\n\nLooking at Gallery.tsx, the carousel items have fixed sizes (w-72 h-48 md:w-80 md:h-56, w-40 h-40 md:w-48 md:h-48, w-64 md:w-72) so they should have fixed dimensions which prevent layout shift.\n\nThe only thing I can see that might need refinement is:\n1. Ensure the carousel items have proper dimensions to prevent CLS\n2. Make sure all acceptance criteria are properly addressed\n\nLet me check what needs to be done. The task says the story is currently not marked as \"passes: true\" (passes: false). So I need to complete the implementation to satisfy all acceptance criteria.\n\nLooking more carefully at the CSS and HTML:\n\n\nThe CSS is aspirational for future Chrome 135+ support. Since the pseudo-elements don't actually exist yet, the native buttons and dots won't render. The Gallery.tsx currently relies on manual JavaScript buttons, which means the native pseudo-elements need to be hidden or removed when support arrives. For now, browsers without ::scroll-button() support will use the @supports not block, but the JS buttons remain necessary since the pseudo-elements aren't available.\n\nThe real question is what the story actually requires me to implement. These pseudo-elements don't exist in any browser yet—Chrome 135 (March 2025) is when they'll ship. The @supports (selector(::scroll-button(*))) guard indicates the code should prepare for this future feature. The CSS is already set up with the guard, Gallery.tsx applies the carousel-scroll-container class, and the manual JS buttons serve as the fallback. What's likely missing is visual styling or proper fallback behavior that would cause acceptance criteria to fail.\n\nLooking at the requirements more carefully: AC1 expects scroll-snap CSS with ::scroll-button() styles for controls, which the CSS has. AC2 requires ::scroll-marker styles for keyboard-accessible indicators. The issue might be that the pseudo-element styles lack proper visual representation or the fallback doesn't fully satisfy the acceptance criteria without the actual browser implementation.\n\nSince Chrome 135+ hasn't released yet, I can't verify if the pseudo-elements render correctly. The implementation appears structurally sound—Gallery.tsx uses the carousel-scroll-container class, scroll-snap is configured, and CSS rules exist for both button and marker pseudo-elements. But without browser support, I can't confirm whether the dots actually display or if keyboard navigation works as intended. The story might be incomplete because it depends on Chrome 135+ release, or there's a gap between what the CSS defines and what the acceptance criteria actually require.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":8,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1392,"cache_read_input_tokens":86460,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1392},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"650472e5-a1e2-490b-836b-3f33e6c52d9d","uuid":"b62fdb4e-ef9e-4336-87ee-3d11a4fb90b0"}
